# =============================================================================
# not-uber-service — MASTER SETTINGS FILE
# =============================================================================
# Copy to .env and edit:   make init   (or: cp .env.example .env)
#
# This one file configures the whole stack. The root docker-compose.yaml
# includes every component without an `env_file:`, so all fourteen of them
# resolve their ${...} from here. The per-component .env.example files stay
# as the reference for what each component consumes, and are what a
# standalone single-component run uses; they play no part in the full stack.
#
# THE ONLY THING YOU MUST EDIT IS SECTION 1. Everything below it has a
# working default and can be left alone on a first deployment.
#
# Values may reference values defined ABOVE them (${LIKE_THIS}), which is how
# the three names PostgreSQL's password goes by stay one secret.
#
# `make preflight` refuses to deploy while any change-me placeholder is left.
# =============================================================================


# =============================================================================
# 1. SECRETS — CHANGE EVERY ONE OF THESE
# =============================================================================
# Nothing here has a safe default on purpose: the compose files use
# ${VAR:?...} so a missing value fails immediately instead of starting a
# cluster with a password somebody could guess.

# PostgreSQL superuser. Created by Patroni on first bootstrap, then used by
# bootstrap, the generators, dispatch, city-service and Debezium.
PG_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD=change-me
# Replication between the Patroni nodes. Never used by an application.
PG_REPLICATION_PASSWORD=change-me-too
# Guards Patroni's REST API: switchover, failover, restart, reload. The
# read-only endpoints stay open, because the HAProxy checks depend on them.
PATRONI_REST_PASSWORD=change-me-rest

# Redis. One password for the data nodes, used by every service.
REDIS_PASSWORD=change-me-redis

# ClickHouse. Created by the image on first start; Grafana, Superset,
# bootstrap and the sink all log in with it.
CH_PASSWORD=change-me-clickhouse

# Grafana admin login.
GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me-grafana

# Superset admin login, and the key that signs its session cookies.
# Losing SUPERSET_SECRET_KEY means saved database passwords can no longer be
# decrypted, so keep it with the rest of these.
SUPERSET_ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me-superset
SUPERSET_SECRET_KEY=change-me-superset-secret

# ---- derived from the above; do not edit ------------------------------------
# PostgreSQL's password reaches its three consumers under three different
# names. They must be the same secret, so they are written once above and
# referenced here rather than repeated.
PG_PASSWORD=${PG_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD}
CDC_PG_PASSWORD=${PG_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD}


# =============================================================================
# 2. STACK-WIDE
# =============================================================================
# Every container runs UTC, and every stored timestamp is UTC.
TZ=UTC

# Reserved, and NOT yet wired up: no component reads this today. The demand
# curve lives as a fixed hour-by-hour table in the generators and is indexed
# by the UTC hour, so the busy hours currently fall at UTC rush hour rather
# than New York's. Set this when the pacing config it belongs to exists.
SIM_TIMEZONE=America/New_York

# How much the Python components say. DEBUG on the whole stack is a lot;
# prefer restarting one container with it.
LOG_LEVEL=INFO


# =============================================================================
# 3. HOST PORTS (lb-a / lb-b entry tier)
# =============================================================================
# Component containers publish nothing. Everything enters through the pair.
# lb-b is the failover twin and offsets every port by +10000, because a host
# cannot bind the same port twice. Change these if something on the host
# already holds one — `make preflight` tells you which.

LB_A_PG_WRITE_PORT=5432
LB_A_PG_READ_PORT=5433
LB_A_STATS_PORT=8404
LB_A_CH_HTTP_PORT=8123
LB_A_CH_NATIVE_PORT=9000
LB_A_GRAFANA_PORT=3000
LB_A_SUPERSET_PORT=8088

LB_B_PG_WRITE_PORT=15432
LB_B_PG_READ_PORT=15433
LB_B_STATS_PORT=18404
LB_B_CH_HTTP_PORT=18123
LB_B_CH_NATIVE_PORT=19000
LB_B_GRAFANA_PORT=13000
LB_B_SUPERSET_PORT=18088


# =============================================================================
# 4. IMAGES (all pinned to an exact version)
# =============================================================================
# `make pull` fetches every one of these and is the first thing to run on a
# new host: a tag that has moved or been withdrawn should surface then, not
# halfway through a bring-up.
#
# Every tag here names an exact release. A floating tag like `redis:8.2` or
# `python:3.13-slim` would mean two builds months apart quietly produce
# different images, which is the opposite of what this repository is for.

# The version stamped on the eleven images this repository builds. It is the
# STACK's version, not any base image's — which base each one was built on is
# recorded in its org.opencontainers.image.base.name label, so:
#   docker image inspect nus/superset:0.1.0 \
#     -f '{{index .Config.Labels "org.opencontainers.image.base.name"}}'
NUS_VERSION=0.1.0

# Entry tier.
HAPROXY_IMAGE=haproxy:3.4.4

# a-infra-postgres. PostGIS and pgRouting are built on top of this base.
PG_IMAGE=postgres:18.6
ETCD_IMAGE=quay.io/coreos/etcd:v3.6.14
OPENSSL_IMAGE=alpine/openssl:3.5.8

# b-infra-redis.
REDIS_IMAGE=redis:8.10.1

# c-infra-kafka. The Schema Registry and the Avro converter Debezium uses
# are both Confluent components and must stay on the same major line.
KAFKA_IMAGE=apache/kafka:4.3.1
SCHEMA_REGISTRY_IMAGE=confluentinc/cp-schema-registry:8.3.1
AVRO_CONVERTER_VERSION=8.3.1
BUSYBOX_IMAGE=busybox:1.38.0

# d-infra-debezium.
CONNECT_IMAGE=quay.io/debezium/connect:3.6.2.Final

# e-infra-clickhouse. 26.3 is a Long-Term Support line — ClickHouse marks
# one in March and one in August each year and supports it for twelve
# months. Server and Keeper must be the same version.
CH_SERVER_IMAGE=clickhouse/clickhouse-server:26.3.28.5
CH_KEEPER_IMAGE=clickhouse/clickhouse-keeper:26.3.28.5

# f-infra-grafana. The plugin is baked into the image at build time, so
# Grafana never reaches the internet at start-up.
GRAFANA_IMAGE=grafana/grafana:12.4.10
GRAFANA_CLICKHOUSE_PLUGIN_VERSION=4.21.2

# g-infra-superset. The ClickHouse driver is added to Superset's own
# virtualenv at build time — see g-infra-superset/Dockerfile for why that
# distinction matters.
SUPERSET_IMAGE=apache/superset:6.1.0
CLICKHOUSE_CONNECT_VERSION=1.7.2


# =============================================================================
# 5. a-infra-postgres — the OLTP cluster
# =============================================================================
# The Patroni cluster name and the etcd cluster it elects its leader in.
# Non-secret etcd settings live in a-infra-postgres/etcd.env (committed).
PATRONI_SCOPE=nus-pg
PATRONI_REST_USER=patroni

# How everything reaches PostgreSQL: through the proxy, never a pg-* node by
# name. The leader is elected and moves; only the proxies know where it is.
PG_HOST=lb-a
PG_WRITE_PORT=5432
PG_READ_PORT=5433
PG_DATABASE=postgres
PG_USER=postgres


# =============================================================================
# 6. b-infra-redis — the cache
# =============================================================================
# The primary is elected by Sentinel. REDIS_MASTER_HOST is only the seed
# primary of a cold start; after the first failover the nodes' own rewritten
# config decides, and changing this has no further effect.
REDIS_MASTER_NAME=nus-cache
REDIS_MASTER_HOST=redis-1
# Below the 4 GB container limit on purpose: an AOF rewrite forks, and with
# no swap there is nothing to absorb the spike.
REDIS_MAXMEMORY=2560mb
SENTINEL_QUORUM=2
SENTINEL_DOWN_AFTER_MS=5000
SENTINEL_FAILOVER_TIMEOUT_MS=15000

# How the services find Redis: they ask Sentinel who is primary right now.
REDIS_SENTINELS=sentinel-1:26379,sentinel-2:26379,sentinel-3:26379


# =============================================================================
# 7. c-infra-kafka — the streaming backbone
# =============================================================================
# Generated once and then never changed: every broker in one cluster must
# share this id, and changing it on a cluster that holds data orphans it.
KAFKA_CLUSTER_ID=2DgQxcGwSsWn1QTQzKeEFg
# The user inside the Kafka image, which the one-shot hands the volumes to.
# Confirm with:  docker run --rm apache/kafka:4.0.0 id
KAFKA_UID=1000
KAFKA_GID=1000
KAFKA_RETENTION_HOURS=48

KAFKA_BOOTSTRAP=kafka-1:9092,kafka-2:9092,kafka-3:9092
SCHEMA_REGISTRY_URL=http://schema-registry:8081


# =============================================================================
# 8. d-infra-debezium — change data capture
# =============================================================================
# Debezium takes a single hostname, so it cannot fail over from lb-a to lb-b
# by itself. Documented in d-infra-debezium/README.md.
CDC_PG_HOST=lb-a
CDC_PG_PORT=5432
CDC_PG_DATABASE=postgres
CDC_PG_USER=postgres


# =============================================================================
# 9. e-infra-clickhouse — the analytics database
# =============================================================================
CH_USER=nus
CH_DATABASE=nus
# Through the entry tier, so queries and inserts spread over healthy nodes.
CH_HOST=lb-a
CH_HTTP_PORT=8123


# =============================================================================
# 10. f-infra-grafana / g-infra-superset — the dashboards
# =============================================================================
GRAFANA_ADMIN_USER=admin
SUPERSET_ADMIN_USER=admin
SUPERSET_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@example.invalid


# =============================================================================
# 11. THE SIMULATED CITY
# =============================================================================
# A box around New York, divided into a grid of zones. bootstrap and the four
# services that place anything on the map all read these, so they draw the
# same city. Changing them after bootstrap has run means the seeded week and
# the live traffic disagree about where things are — reseed if you do.
CITY_MIN_LAT=40.49
CITY_MAX_LAT=40.92
CITY_MIN_LON=-74.26
CITY_MAX_LON=-73.70
CITY_GRID_ROWS=6
CITY_GRID_COLS=6


# =============================================================================
# 12. h-bootstrap — what gets created, once
# =============================================================================
SEED_DRIVERS=800
SEED_PASSENGERS=5000
HISTORY_DAYS=7
HISTORY_TRIPS_PER_DAY=2000
HISTORY_POSITIONS_PER_TRIP=8

# The street map. The download is skipped when the file is already on the
# volume and matches its published md5, so repeated runs never re-fetch it.
OSM_URL=https://download.geofabrik.de/north-america/us/new-york-latest.osm.pbf
OSM_MD5_URL=https://download.geofabrik.de/north-america/us/new-york-latest.osm.pbf.md5
# Leave false. True skips the map import, and routing does not work without it.
SKIP_OSM_IMPORT=false
# Run the seeding steps again on a database that already has data. Existing
# rows are still kept; this only lifts the "already prepared" short-circuit.
FORCE_RESEED=false


# =============================================================================
# 13. PRICES
# =============================================================================
# Read by bootstrap AND dispatch, so live trips are priced the way the seeded
# week was. Changing one without the other makes the history disagree with
# the present — which is why they are one setting here.
FARE_BASE=3.0
FARE_PER_KM=1.75
FARE_PER_MINUTE=0.45


# =============================================================================
# 14. PACING — the volume dials
# =============================================================================
# These decide how many messages a second the stack has to carry. If it
# struggles, turn these down; never remove containers. DRIVER_TICK_SECONDS
# and TRIP_REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE are the two that matter most.

# j-service-driver — a position per online driver per tick.
DRIVER_TICK_SECONDS=3.0
DRIVER_SPEED_KMH=25.0
DRIVER_ONLINE_SHARE=0.6
DRIVER_SHIFT_CHANGE_CHANCE=0.01
DRIVER_DB_SYNC_SECONDS=30.0
DRIVER_HOTSPOT_REFRESH_SECONDS=60.0

# k-service-passenger — every request becomes a routing query in dispatch,
# which is the most expensive step anywhere in the pipeline.
PASSENGER_TICK_SECONDS=5.0
TRIP_REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE=40.0

# l-service-dispatch
DISPATCH_TICK_SECONDS=1.0
DISPATCH_SEARCH_RADIUS_KM=5.0
DISPATCH_MAX_REQUESTS_PER_TICK=50
CANCEL_BY_DRIVER_CHANCE=0.06
CANCEL_BY_PASSENGER_CHANCE=0.07
TRIP_ACTIVE_TTL_SECONDS=7200

# m-service-city
CITY_SCORE_SECONDS=30.0
CITY_TRAFFIC_UPDATE_MINUTES=5.0
# Six hours: the length of one part of the day, so a score cannot outlive
# the period it describes.
HOTSPOT_TTL_SECONDS=21600
# Read by city-service AND the sink, so "is this a hotspot" means the same
# thing to the one that writes the score and the one that files it.
HOTSPOT_SCORE_THRESHOLD=0.6

# n-service-clickhouse-sink — the time limit is what keeps dashboards close
# to live when the city is quiet and a batch would take minutes to fill.
SINK_BATCH_ROWS=5000
SINK_FLUSH_SECONDS=5.0
SINK_HOTSPOT_REFRESH_SECONDS=30.0

# i-service-cache-updater
CDC_TOPIC_PATTERN=^cdc\..*
CACHE_BATCH_SIZE=500
CACHE_FLUSH_SECONDS=2.0

# Same seed in all three generators, so a run can be repeated exactly.
RANDOM_SEED=20250824


# =============================================================================
# 15. KAFKA CONSUMER GROUPS
# =============================================================================
# One per service, and they MUST stay distinct. Two services sharing a group
# do not each get a copy of the stream — Kafka splits the partitions between
# them, so each silently receives a fraction of what it expects and drops the
# rest. That is why these are six separate names rather than one setting.
#
# Renaming one makes that service re-read from its topics' start.
CACHE_UPDATER_GROUP_ID=cache-updater
DRIVER_GROUP_ID=driver-service
PASSENGER_GROUP_ID=passenger-service
DISPATCH_GROUP_ID=dispatch-service
CITY_GROUP_ID=city-service
SINK_GROUP_ID=clickhouse-sink
